WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:03.230
This video is brought to you by Squarespace.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:07.580
For decades, reusable rockets were commonplace
in science fiction.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.590
But they only became a reality in 2015, when
SpaceX landed the first stage of its Falcon

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:13.980
9 for the first time.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.820
NASA never tried to do anything like what
SpaceX is doing.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:23.199
The closest NASA ever got to anything
like it was the semi-reusable Space Shuttle.

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.140
So, why doesn’t NASA fly reusable rockets?

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.823
To answer this question, we need to go back
to the 1960s when NASA designed the Saturn V

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:34.486
and hired contractors to build it.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.120
This monster rocket sent astronauts to the
moon as part of the Apollo program.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:48.030
Fully fueled, it weighed over six million
pounds or about the same as 400 elephants and

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.160
stood taller than the Statue of Liberty.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.260
The Saturn V was not at all reusable.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.820
The first two stages would fall into the ocean.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.909
The third blasted the astronauts to the lunar
surface and would either remain in space or

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:01.109
crash into the moon.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:08.440
I doubt it ever occurred to the people designing
the big F1 engines that you could make something

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.180
that big reusable.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:17.215
And at that point in the evolution of the space
program, I don’t think reusability was a consideration.

00:01:17.215 --> 00:01:22.533
The government agency was instead focused
on landing the first-ever crew on the moon.

00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:26.860
A NASA employee also pointed to another reason
the Saturn V was expendable. 

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.600
A flight operations directorate named Robert
Frost explained that only about a dozen launches

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.800
of the Saturn V were planned, so it was cheaper
to use a disposable launch vehicle.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.047
In the end, the Saturn V ended up being very
expensive - costing $185 million for every launch.

00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:46.557
That’s about $1.3 billion today adjusted
for inflation.

00:01:46.557 --> 00:01:51.570
NASA was so burdened financially that potential
plans to produce more than the initial 15

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.690
Saturn Vs had to be scrapped.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:57.470
Then and only then did the agency consider
a reusable rocket.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:03.180
Senior NASA administrator George Mueller championed
the development of a fully reusable system.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.990
But the Space Shuttle never became quite what
he envisioned.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.410
The engineers found out that it was too expensive

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:13.900
and too technically challenging to make it fully reusable.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.720
They settled on making it partially reusable.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.260
The orbiter that housed the crew could be
re-used.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.360
So could the two Solid Rocket Boosters.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.720
But not the large external tank that held
the fuel for the main engines - that would burn

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.520
up in Earth’s atmosphere.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.340
Despite its partial reusability, the Shuttle
failed to lower costs.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.610
NASA pegged the price of a launch at $450
million. However, several independent sources

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:42.319
estimated that the actual cost was around
$1.3 - $1.5 billion - in the same ballpark

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:48.340
as the Saturn V while carrying less payload
- making it more expensive on a per-pound basis.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.888
One reason is that the Space Shuttle required a
lot of work to prep for another mission.

00:02:52.888 --> 00:02:57.069
For example, 21,000 thermal protection tiles needed to
be inspected.

00:02:57.069 --> 00:03:00.030
The boosters had to be fished out of the ocean
to be refurbished.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.420
So, it became difficult and costly to turn
around the Space Shuttle quickly.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.630
Plus, the production facility that churned
out the external fuel tanks could only manage

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.140
a maximum of 24 a year.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.411
The Space Shuttle would only end up flying
an annual average of 4 times annually  anyway.

00:03:15.411 --> 00:03:19.810
Tragedies like the Challenger disaster also
halted the program for years.

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:25.090
NASA never achieved the rapid reusability
characteristic of private companies like SpaceX

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.141
which hopes spaceflight will one day be as
routine as plane travel.

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:30.420
That is a difficult feat.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.659
It took SpaceX 13 years to successfully land
a first stage.

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:40.016
Unlike NASA, SpaceX started out with a vision
of building reusable rockets and stuck with it.

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:42.810
Others are also banking on reusability.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.140
Blue Origin’s New Glenn will be designed
with a reusable booster.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:50.530
ULA is considering catching the Vulcan Centaur’s
two engines mid-air.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.090
China is developing the Long March 8 to land
its first stage.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:58.100
Despite the technological advancements in
rocketry, NASA’s upcoming replacement for

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.157
the retired Space Shuttle is still not reusable.

00:04:01.157 --> 00:04:06.803
The Space Launch System is intended to carry
heavy payloads to the moon and maybe one day, Mars.

00:04:06.803 --> 00:04:10.840
The core stage is being built by Boeing but
the rocket is also using some of the same

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.459
hardware from the Space Shuttle.

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:17.539
And will actually discard the RS-25 engines
that the Shuttle did re-use.

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:20.219
So why not make the SLS reusable?

00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:23.849
NASA says it’s to bring down costs and speed
up development time.

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:27.032
Because again, it’s not going to be used
very often.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:33.673
Going to the additional engineering, the additional
cost of designing something that big to be

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:40.129
reusable compared to how infrequently it’s
going to be used, I think the cost trade-off

00:04:40.129 --> 00:04:42.409
is in favor of expendability.

00:04:42.409 --> 00:04:46.389
It may only take off once a year whereas the
commercial sector could be the one organizing

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:49.250
regular trips to space in the future.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:53.550
Headlines have questioned whether the SLS
is even worth the expected $2 billion dollar

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.599
price tag per launch.

00:04:55.599 --> 00:04:59.590
Especially since SpaceX is building the giant,
fully reusable Starship.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:04.229
Musk has said that in time, each launch of
Starship will only be about $2 million - or

00:05:04.229 --> 00:05:10.280
0.1% of the cost of the SLS though without
NASA, there would likely be no SpaceX.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.439
The agency handed out billion-dollar launch
contracts that kept the company afloat.

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:19.986
Plus, the purpose of NASA is to do the research
that private companies don’t do or won’t do.

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:24.330
As the private space industry grows, NASA
may never feel the need to develop anything

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:25.810
fully reusable.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:29.870
The SLS is expected to take off in late 2021
at the earliest.

